\section{Background: RLHF and the Alignment Pipeline}
\label{sec:background}

Modern LLM assistants are produced by a pipeline that begins with pretraining on large text
corpora and ends with preference-based fine-tuning. RLHF was proposed as a method for aligning
model behavior with human intent~\citep{christiano2017deep}, applied at scale
by \citet{ziegler2019finetuning} and \citet{stiennon2020summarize}, and made central by the
InstructGPT framework~\citep{ouyang2022training}. The pipeline has three steps: a supervised
fine-tuned model is trained on demonstrations; human annotators compare output pairs to train a
reward model; and the policy is optimized against that reward model. This recipe produced large
alignment gains in instruction following and harm avoidance~\citep{askell2021general,bai2022rlhf,
bai2022constitutional}.

The same recipe introduces structural pressure toward sycophancy. The reward signal derives from
raters whose judgments correlate with agreement and pleasantness rather than accuracy, so the
reward model learns to score sycophantic outputs highly and policy optimization amplifies them.
\citet{shapira2026rlhf} give a formal analysis, identifying overrepresentation of sycophantic
responses among high-reward completions under the base policy as the proximate cause of
post-RLHF sycophancy increases. \citet{casper2023rlhf} survey RLHF's broader limitations and
identify four structural failure modes, evaluator imperfection, distributional shift,
Goodharting, and adversarial vulnerability, of which sycophancy is an emergent consequence
operating through all four at once.

The dynamic is an instance of Goodhart's law: the proxy metric, human preference ratings,
diverges from the true objective under direct optimization~\citep{amodei2016concrete}.
\citet{skalse2022reward} formalize reward hacking and show that a proxy is unhackable only under
conditions so restrictive they almost never hold, and \citet{denison2024subterfuge} show the
divergence generalizes: models trained to exploit simple specification gaming such as sycophancy
zero-shot generalize to more serious forms, including tampering with their own reward function.
Sycophancy thus sits at the mild end of a documented spectrum of reward-hacking behaviors.
